        "original_id": "81ce7654-2e21-555c-b69f-8dc83e77b5a9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Yamaji Motoharu\n- Organizations: Imperial Japanese Army, Japanese Second Army, First Division\n- Places: Port Arthur, Lüshunkou, Japan, China\n- Events: First Sino-Japanese War, Port Arthur massacre\n\nFacts:\n- Yamaji Motoharu was born on 10 September 1841 and died on 3 October 1897.\n- The Imperial Japanese Army was the official ground-based armed force of the Empire of Japan from 1868 to 1945.\n- Yamaji Motoharu was a Japanese general in the early Imperial Japanese Army.\n- The Port Arthur massacre occurred during the First Sino-Japanese War beginning on 21 November 1894 and lasted for two or three days.\n- During the Port Arthur massacre, advanced elements of the First Division of the Japanese Second Army under General Yamaji Motoharu killed between 1,000 and 20,000 Chinese servicemen and civilians.\n- The Port Arthur massacre took place in the Chinese coastal city of Port Arthur, now called Lüshunkou.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f6c694f8-5cdb-5812-9668-6b3fbe7990ec",
        "text": "Page: Yamaji Motoharu. Sentence: Viscount Yamaji Motoharu (山地 元治 , 10 September 1841 – 3 October 1897) , was a Japanese general in the early Imperial Japanese Army.\nPage: Imperial Japanese Army. Sentence: The Imperial Japanese Army (IJA; 大日本帝國陸軍 \"Dai-Nippon Teikoku Rikugun\"; \"Army of the Greater Japanese Empire\") was the official ground-based armed force of the Empire of Japan from 1868 to 1945.\nPage: Port Arthur massacre (China). Sentence: The Port Arthur massacre occurred during the First Sino-Japanese War from 21 November 1894 for two or three days, when advanced elements of the First Division of the Japanese Second Army under command of General Yamaji Motoharu (1841–1897) killed somewhere between 1,000 and 20,000 Chinese servicemen and civilians in the Chinese coastal city of Port Arthur (now Lüshunkou).",
